WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.080
Picture a town park, a wide lawn between the houses and the station.

2
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:08.715
People cut across it.

3
00:00:08.715 --> 00:00:11.815
And soon, muddy tracks run in every direction.

4
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:14.077
The council puts up a sign.

5
00:00:14.077 --> 00:00:15.514
Keep off the grass.

6
00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:16.725
Nobody reads it.

7
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:20.331
The next spring, a new park keeper tries something else.

8
00:00:20.331 --> 00:00:24.456
Paved paths, exactly where the tracks were, with lights and benches.

9
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:26.770
Within a month, the mud is gone.

10
00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:28.413
And nobody needed a sign.

11
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:30.445
Rules feel like the quick fix.

12
00:00:30.445 --> 00:00:34.249
A path people want to walk takes longer to build, and it works.

13
00:00:34.249 --> 00:00:36.483
So what has this to do with software?

14
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.950
List what hello-devops needs by now.

15
00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:48.343
A Dockerfile and workflows, Kubernetes YAML and Argo CD, metrics, dashboards, logs, tracing, policies and secrets.

16
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.790
You learned all of it in this course.

17
00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:54.276
Now picture forty product teams, each building that alone.

18
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.934
They solve the same problems forty ways, and whoever is on call learns every one.

19
00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:00.807
Muddy tracks.

20
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:04.330
The tempting fix is the sign: a mandatory standard.

21
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:08.727
Teams use it because a manager said so, and look for ways around it.

22
00:01:08.933 --> 00:01:12.970
Or buy a developer portal, a website listing every service.

23
00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.391
Picture one that, six months later, few teams use.

24
00:01:16.391 --> 00:01:17.828
Pause and guess: why?

25
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:24.043
Usually because there was little real self-service behind it.

26
00:01:24.043 --> 00:01:26.804
A beautiful map of paths that don't exist.

27
00:01:26.804 --> 00:01:28.053
Nobody walks a map.

28
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:30.700
What works is the paved path.

29
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.241
Most large companies call the work platform engineering.

30
00:01:34.241 --> 00:01:39.489
A platform team builds self-service tools, so product teams can ship by themselves.

31
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.328
Self-service means you get what you need yourself, at once.

32
00:01:43.328 --> 00:01:46.156
No ticket, no waiting a week for another team.

33
00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:53.069
Its main product is the golden path, a ready-made way to do a common job, like create a new service.

34
00:01:53.069 --> 00:01:58.028
Two promises: compliant, with security built in, and tested, kept working.

35
00:01:58.233 --> 00:01:59.183
And the portal?

36
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:01.209
That's the map at the park gate.

37
00:02:01.209 --> 00:02:04.312
Useful, but the platform is the paths themselves.

38
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:06.133
Let's pave one.

39
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:08.266
Mark hello-devops as a template.

40
00:02:08.266 --> 00:02:11.265
Then count the files that hard-code its name.

41
00:02:11.265 --> 00:02:13.930
Twenty-seven, in the core folders alone.

42
00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.729
Each one is a line a new team must change.

43
00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:18.510
Now play the new team.

44
00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:25.320
Create payments-api from the template, replace the old name everywhere, and check none is left.

45
00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:25.679
Zero.

46
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.443
Run the same checks as the original.

47
00:02:28.443 --> 00:02:30.351
Pause and guess: all green?

48
00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:35.148
One fails, and it's a real template bug.

49
00:02:35.148 --> 00:02:39.407
The rename changed the chart inside its files, but not its folder.

50
00:02:39.633 --> 00:02:41.685
Fix it, and fix the template too.

51
00:02:41.685 --> 00:02:42.493
Eight passed.

52
00:02:42.493 --> 00:02:46.907
That failure was a fresh muddy track, showing where a path was missing.

53
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.090
Paths carry one more thing: the bill.

54
00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:54.486
The park pays one water bill, and nobody knows which bed drinks most.

55
00:02:54.486 --> 00:02:57.097
A cloud bill arrives with no names on it.

56
00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.267
FinOps makes that cost visible, and it starts with an owner for every cost.

57
00:03:02.267 --> 00:03:05.313
In Kubernetes, a team label on every workload.

58
00:03:05.533 --> 00:03:13.497
A cost tool splits each node's price between its Pods, by what they request, or use if that's more, then adds up by label.

59
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:14.580
Why requests?

60
00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.018
Because the scheduler reserves them.

61
00:03:17.018 --> 00:03:21.080
You pay for what you set aside, even when the CPUs sit idle.

62
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.149
And the line people forget: capacity nobody requested still costs money.

63
00:03:26.149 --> 00:03:29.719
That's idle cost, sprinklers watering an empty field.

64
00:03:29.719 --> 00:03:31.133
Give it an owner too.

65
00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:34.624
Show each team its share while the money stays central.

66
00:03:34.624 --> 00:03:36.756
That's showback, and it comes first.

67
00:03:37.567 --> 00:03:40.271
Totals grow with the business, so try this.

68
00:03:40.271 --> 00:03:43.415
Traffic doubles, and the bill grows forty percent.

69
00:03:43.415 --> 00:03:45.931
Did each request get cheaper, or dearer?

70
00:03:45.931 --> 00:03:46.937
Pause and guess.

71
00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:49.703
Cheaper.

72
00:03:49.703 --> 00:03:54.997
One point four times the cost, for two times the requests, is zero point seven.

73
00:03:54.997 --> 00:03:59.555
The bill went up, and each request got about thirty percent cheaper.

74
00:03:59.767 --> 00:04:03.539
That's a unit cost, and hello-devops already has both inputs.

75
00:04:03.539 --> 00:04:07.929
Prometheus counts its requests, and the team label says whose they are.

76
00:04:08.733 --> 00:04:13.554
The honest limits: each tool solves a pain you feel only at some size.

77
00:04:13.554 --> 00:04:14.174
One team?

78
00:04:14.174 --> 00:04:16.378
A template repository is enough.

79
00:04:16.378 --> 00:04:18.720
A portal pays off with many teams.

80
00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.268
A cost tool needs owner labels first.

81
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.887
And watch where people leave the path.

82
00:04:23.887 --> 00:04:26.148
If many teams do, the path is wrong.

83
00:04:26.148 --> 00:04:29.477
That's the most useful feedback a platform team gets.

84
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.247
The moral of the story: build the path people want to walk.

85
00:04:34.247 --> 00:04:36.589
A rule alone won't keep them on it.

86
00:04:36.589 --> 00:04:38.729
Self-service, compliant, tested.

87
00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:41.071
Every cost gets an owner, idle too.

88
00:04:41.071 --> 00:04:42.877
And watch cost per request.

89
00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:44.773
One quick check.

90
00:04:44.773 --> 00:04:49.132
Your cluster bill doubled, and nobody knows which team caused it.

91
00:04:49.132 --> 00:04:51.144
Which FinOps step comes first?

92
00:04:51.144 --> 00:04:52.217
Pause and think.

93
00:04:54.433 --> 00:04:57.232
Label every workload with its owning team.

94
00:04:57.232 --> 00:05:00.630
Until a cost has an owner, every saving is a guess.

95
00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:01.963
Thanks for watching.
